From: Claudia Pellegrino <claui@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:42:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Use pre-Python-3.14 forking semantics

Python 3.14 changed the default multiprocessing start method on Linux
from 'fork' to 'forkserver'. That breaks this application in two ways:

1. it now tries to pickle everything, so it fails with:

    > cannot pickle '_thread.lock' object`

2. the child loses all the class-level state the parent had already
fiddled with (e.g. stream server host and port.)

To fix this, reconfigure multiprocessing so it uses the old behavior
from Python 3.13 and earlier.
---
 pulseaudio_dlna/__main__.py | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/pulseaudio_dlna/__main__.py b/pulseaudio_dlna/__main__.py
index 86d7d7a..ae51da8 100644
--- a/pulseaudio_dlna/__main__.py
+++ b/pulseaudio_dlna/__main__.py
@@ -117,6 +117,7 @@ Examples:
 
 import sys
 import os
+import multiprocessing
 import docopt
 import logging
 import socket
@@ -126,6 +127,10 @@ import getpass
 def main(argv=sys.argv[1:]):
 
     import pulseaudio_dlna
+
+    if multiprocessing.get_start_method(allow_none=True) is None:
+        multiprocessing.set_start_method("fork")
+
     options = docopt.docopt(__doc__, version=pulseaudio_dlna.__version__)
 
     level = logging.DEBUG
-- 
